    "doi": "https://doi.org/10.5194/tc-2-67-2008",
    "abstract": "Abstract. The aim of this paper is to describe in detail how the benchmark tests ISMIP-HOM (Ice Sheet Model Intercomparison Project-Higher-Order ice-sheet Model) have been performed using the open source finite element (FE) code Elmer (http://www.csc.fi/elmer). The ISMIP-HOM setup consists of five diagnostic and one prognostic experiments, for both 2-D and 3-D geometries. For all the tests, the full-Stokes equations are solved. Some technical points concerning FE, such as mesh characteristics, stabilisation methods, numerical methods used to solve the linear system and parallel performance are discussed. For all these setups, the CPU time consumption in relation to the accuracy of the solution is analysed. Based on these findings, some general rules on optimising the computing time versus the accuracy of the results are deduced.",
    "field": "Ice sheet modeling",
    "problem": "The paper addresses the challenge of optimizing full-Stokes ice flow model performance, as the trade-off between solution accuracy and computational cost is highly sensitive to technical factors such as mesh design, numerical methods, and convergence criteria used in the simulations.",
    "method": "This paper describes in detail how the ISMIP-HOM benchmark experiments were performed using the finite-element code Elmer to solve the full-Stokes equations, while analyzing the influence of various numerical parameters, such as mesh characteristics and solvers, on the accuracy and computational cost of the solution.",
    "task": "The task is to describe the performance of the finite-element code Elmer in solving the ISMIP-HOM benchmark experiments for ice-sheet models, analyzing the influence of numerical parameters on the solution's accuracy and computational cost.",
    "model": [
        "Elmer"
    ],
